# 1. YOUTUBE LECTURE PROCESSING — UP TO 12 HOURS

## Problem

Currently, the YouTube lecture workflow requires students to paste transcripts manually or has other limitations preventing convenient processing of long lectures.

This creates unnecessary friction.

Many JEE lectures are two, four, six, eight, or even twelve hours long.

Students should not need to manually collect and paste a transcript to use LearnMate.

## Required user experience

The primary workflow must be:

**Paste YouTube URL → Analyze Lecture → Generate Study Material**

The student should only have to paste the video URL and choose the desired output.

Remove the manual transcript-pasting requirement from the primary YouTube learning interface.

Do not make a pasted transcript a prerequisite for generating notes, summaries, chapter breakdowns, or revision material.

## Duration requirements

Design the processing system to support videos with durations up to at least **12 hours (720 minutes)**.

The pipeline must not assume that a lecture is limited to 30, 60, or 120 minutes.

Do not send a twelve-hour transcript or all video-derived material into one LLM prompt.

Implement a scalable processing architecture.

## YouTube input pipeline

Inspect the existing YouTube integration first.

Use official APIs or other permitted, authorized content-access mechanisms.

Important:

- The official YouTube captions download API has authorization limitations.
- Do not assume captions can be downloaded for every public video.
- Do not bypass access restrictions, DRM, or platform authorization.
- Do not use unauthorized scraping or downloading as a hidden fallback.
- Where the user or application has legitimate access to an audio source, an authorized transcription pipeline may be used.
- If an inaccessible video cannot be processed lawfully or technically, explain the reason clearly.
- Never generate fictional lecture notes while claiming the video was analyzed.

The user experience should remain URL-first and should never require manual transcript pasting.

## Long-lecture architecture

Implement or prepare an appropriate backend processing pipeline supporting:

- Video URL validation.
- Metadata retrieval.
- Lecture duration detection.
- Available caption or authorized audio-source discovery.
- Timestamped text processing.
- Transcript/source chunking.
- Overlapping contextual boundaries where appropriate.
- Chunk-level summaries.
- Hierarchical summary aggregation.
- Topic and chapter detection.
- Formula extraction.
- Important concept extraction.
- Timestamp references.
- Checkpointing.
- Resumable processing.
- Cancellation and retry.
- Partial failure recovery.
- Bounded concurrency.
- Provider rate-limit handling.
- Duplicate-job protection.

Processing must not depend on keeping one browser request open for hours.

Use a durable asynchronous job architecture if appropriate for the existing backend.

Provide real progress updates and persist recoverable job state.

Do not display invented percentages or artificial progress.

## Student-facing outputs

For successfully processed lectures, support:

1. Complete structured study notes.
2. Topic-wise lecture breakdown.
3. Important formulas and definitions.
4. Chapter or concept summaries.
5. One-page revision sheets.
6. Practice questions.
7. Flashcards where supported.
8. Handwritten-style notes.
9. Relevant diagram creation.
10. Connections to AI Tutor and Creative Studio.

For accessible source material, allow students to click timestamps to navigate to the corresponding video position.

Where a source chunk is unavailable, do not invent its content.

## Performance and cost controls

Twelve-hour lecture processing may be resource-intensive.

Implement:

- Efficient chunk-level caching.
- Per-user quotas and job limits.
- Reuse of previously processed chunks.
- Controlled provider concurrency.
- Processing cancellation.
- Retry from failed chunks.
- Server-side cost safeguards.
- Fair usage protections.
- Accurate processing status.

Avoid repeatedly processing an entire lecture when a student requests only one section.

## Verification

Test with authorized or controlled fixtures representing:

- 30-minute lecture.
- 2-hour lecture.
- 6-hour lecture.
- 12-hour lecture.
- Video without available captions.
- Invalid or private video.
- Provider failure.
- Interrupted processing.
- Repeated request for an already processed lecture.

For the twelve-hour test, verify full duration coverage and complete chunk processing, not merely URL acceptance.

Clearly distinguish real end-to-end source processing from synthetic long-duration fixture tests.

